% ===== LIT =====
\section{Introduction}
The rapid growth of digital banking, online payments, mobile transactions, and electronic financial services has increased the volume of transaction data that financial institutions must monitor. At the same time, fraudulent behaviour has become increasingly complex, creating a need for detection systems that can process large amounts of data and identify suspicious patterns efficiently.

The research problem considered in this literature review is the application of machine learning algorithms and big data analysis to detect fraudulent transactions in the banking sector. The central challenge is that fraudulent transactions normally represent only a small proportion of all transactions, while fraudulent behaviour can change over time. Consequently, a useful detection system must address class imbalance, changing fraud patterns, false positives, scalability, and the requirement for rapid decision making.

The literature selected for this review contains both empirical research and review studies. The technical studies provide evidence about the implementation and evaluation of particular machine learning, anomaly detection, blockchain, and distributed-processing approaches. The review studies provide broader perspectives on algorithmic trends, research classifications, datasets, evaluation metrics, and unresolved research problems.

This literature review has two objectives. First, it analyses individual technical studies to determine how machine learning and big data techniques have been applied to financial fraud detection. Second, it synthesizes review literature to identify common methodologies, research trends, limitations, and opportunities for future research. The remainder of the paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:lit} discusses the ten technical and ten review articles. Section~\ref{sec:synth} synthesizes common themes and research gaps. Section~\ref{sec:disc} discusses implications for the proposed research. Section~\ref{sec:methods} describes the methods and materials of the proposed study, Section~\ref{sec:results} reports the results, and Section~\ref{sec:concl} concludes.

\section{Technical and Review Articles}\label{sec:lit}
\subsection{Application of Artificial Intelligence for Fraudulent Banking Operations Recognition}
Mytnyk et al.~\cite{mytnyk2023} address the problem of recognizing fraudulent banking operations using artificial intelligence and machine learning. The study is directly related to the banking context because it focuses on transaction records and fraudulent banking operations. The methodology uses machine learning and artificial intelligence models for fraud classification and considers imbalanced transaction data. A stacked generalization approach is examined as part of the detection process. The reported results indicate that stacked generalization achieved an AUC of 0.954. The authors demonstrate that machine learning can distinguish fraudulent from legitimate banking operations. The main contribution is empirical evidence that combining multiple learning models can improve fraud-recognition performance. A strength is the direct focus on banking fraud and performance evaluation, while a limitation is that results obtained from a particular dataset may not automatically generalize to all banking institutions or transaction environments. The article is highly relevant because it supports investigation of ensemble learning as part of a broader big-data fraud detection architecture.

\subsection{Machine Learning and Blockchain Based Fraud Detection}
Ashfaq et al.~\cite{ashfaq2022} investigate fraud and anomaly detection in financial transactions, particularly in the Bitcoin and e-banking context. The problem is identification of fraudulent transaction patterns while improving the security of the transaction environment. The methodology combines machine learning with blockchain technology. XGBoost and Random Forest are used to classify transaction patterns, while blockchain and smart-contract mechanisms are incorporated into the proposed security architecture. Precision and AUC are among the evaluation measures. The study demonstrates that machine learning can be integrated with blockchain-based transaction security. Its strength is the combination of predictive detection with transaction integrity. However, the experimental environment is particularly focused on Bitcoin transactions, so direct generalization to conventional banking databases requires additional investigation. The study remains relevant because banking-fraud detection can benefit from predictive analytics and secure transaction infrastructures, although blockchain is not the primary focus of the proposed research.

\subsection{Performance of Different Machine Learning Algorithms in Detecting Financial Fraud}
Alsuwailem et al.~\cite{alsuwailem2023} study the detection of money laundering using supervised machine learning in Saudi Arabia. The research addresses proactive identification of financial crime using transaction and establishment-level information. Random Forest, Decision Tree, Gradient Boosting, and K-Nearest Neighbor models are evaluated using accuracy, precision, recall, F-measure, and AUC. At the establishment level, Random Forest achieved 93\% accuracy, while Decision Tree, Gradient Boosting, and KNN achieved 90\%, 74\%, and 87\%, respectively. At the annual level, Decision Tree and Random Forest both achieved 98\% accuracy. The study contributes empirical evidence that different algorithms perform differently depending on the structure of financial data. Its strength is the use of several algorithms and evaluation metrics. Its limitation is the specific Saudi institutional context and money-laundering problem. The article is relevant because it demonstrates the importance of comparing several algorithms rather than assuming that one model is universally appropriate.

\subsection{Internet Financial Fraud Identification and User Behaviour}
Xiong et al.~\cite{xiong2022} investigate internet financial fraud identification in a big-data environment and examine factors affecting user behaviour and trust. The study constructs a fraud-identification model using a Back Propagation Neural Network and compares its performance with Support Vector Machine and Random Forest. A structural equation model is also used to investigate relationships involving perceived security, authentication, privacy, transaction non-repudiation, and data integrity. The BPNN-based fraud-identification model achieved a reported precision of 88.14\%, accuracy of 96.37\%, and recall of 70.96\%. The contribution is the integration of technical fraud detection with behavioural and security factors. A strength is that the study does not treat fraud detection as purely an algorithmic problem. However, the results are influenced by the particular internet-finance environment investigated. The study is relevant because banking fraud systems must consider both transaction characteristics and the broader security environment in which customers operate.

\subsection{Credit Card Fraud and Class Imbalance}
Chang et al.~\cite{chang2024} focus on fraud detection in highly imbalanced credit-card transaction data. The research addresses the problem that legitimate transactions substantially outnumber fraudulent transactions. The methodology compares random under-sampling with oversampling using SMOTE. Machine learning models are evaluated using accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score. Random under-sampling achieved a recall of 92.86\% but at the cost of lower precision. SMOTE achieved 86.75\% accuracy and an F1-score of 73.47\%, with somewhat lower recall. These results demonstrate the trade-off between identifying more fraudulent cases and maintaining precision. The main contribution is evidence concerning the class-imbalance problem. A strength is the explicit comparison of data-balancing techniques on imbalanced transaction data. A limitation is that balancing strategies may produce different results depending on the dataset and fraud distribution. The study is highly relevant because class imbalance is one of the central problems in banking fraud detection.

\subsection{Big Data-Driven Distributed Machine Learning}
Theodorakopoulos et al.~\cite{theodorakopoulos2025} address the scalability problem associated with large credit-card transaction datasets. The study implements machine learning within a PySpark-based distributed processing framework. Logistic Regression, Decision Trees, Random Forest, XGBoost, and CatBoost are evaluated using accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, specificity, Brier score, and latency. XGBoost achieved 99.97\% test accuracy and a Brier score of 0.0002, while CatBoost achieved 99.96\% test accuracy and a Brier score of 0.0004. The study also reports the ability of PySpark to support distributed processing and low-latency analysis. The main contribution is the integration of advanced machine learning with distributed big-data processing. A major strength is that scalability and predictive performance are evaluated together. Potential limitations include computational requirements, possible overfitting in highly accurate models, and the need for further testing in live banking environments. This article is particularly relevant because it directly connects machine learning algorithms with big-data processing infrastructure.

\subsection{Comparative Analysis of Fraudulent Banking Transaction Models}
Preciado Mart\'inez et al.~\cite{preciado2025} investigate fraudulent banking transactions using real-world transfer data. The principal problem is identifying suspicious transactions in a highly imbalanced financial dataset. The authors compare Random Forest, Neural Networks, and Naive Bayes. The dataset contains approximately 565,000 real-world transfers. Random Forest achieved 100\% accuracy for legitimate transactions and 95.79\% accuracy for fraud detection in the reported evaluation. The findings support the feasibility of machine learning for transaction monitoring. The strength of this study is the direct use of banking transfer data and comparison of several models. A limitation is that results from a single dataset may not represent all banking systems and transaction types. The article is directly relevant because it examines fraudulent banking transactions and provides a comparative basis for selecting machine learning models.

\subsection{Fraud Detection Using Machine Learning and Big Data Analytics}
Byrapu Reddy et al.~\cite{byrapureddy2024} examine fraud detection in e-commerce using machine learning, artificial intelligence, and big data analytics. Although the context is e-commerce rather than traditional banking, the transaction-monitoring problem is closely related. The study proposes the use of a big-data platform for processing large amounts of online transaction information. Machine learning and AI are considered for pattern recognition, anomaly detection, predictive modelling, and fraud prevention. The study concludes that large-scale data analytics and machine learning can support proactive fraud identification by finding patterns and anomalies within massive transaction datasets. Its strength is the emphasis on the connection between big-data infrastructure and fraud analytics. Its main limitation for the current research is the e-commerce rather than banking setting. Nevertheless, it is relevant because modern banking systems also generate high-volume digital transaction streams that require scalable processing technologies.

\subsection{Anomaly Detection in Financial Data}
Bakumenko and Elragal~\cite{bakumenko2022} address the detection of financial anomalies and fraudulent or erroneous financial records. The research is motivated by the difficulty of manually identifying irregularities in large financial datasets. The study investigates both supervised and unsupervised machine learning approaches for identifying anomalies in financial data, including general-ledger and accounting information. The findings demonstrate the usefulness of machine learning for automating anomaly detection and supporting financial auditing processes. The research also highlights the importance of identifying unusual observations rather than relying only on predefined fraud rules. A strength is the consideration of both supervised and unsupervised approaches. A limitation is that accounting anomaly detection is not identical to real-time banking transaction fraud detection. The article is relevant because anomaly detection is important when labelled fraudulent transactions are scarce.

\subsection{Empirical Evaluation of Machine Learning Techniques for Financial Fraud Detection}
Kamal~\cite{kamal2023} investigates machine learning techniques for financial fraud detection using transaction-level data. The study addresses the practical difficulty of balancing fraud detection with false-alert workload in financial institutions. The methodology combines practitioner survey information with transaction records and evaluates data quality, system integration, analytics competency, model interpretability, management support, and compliance readiness. Machine learning models are evaluated using precision, recall, F1-score, ROC AUC, cross-validation, and threshold sensitivity. XGBoost achieved a reported precision of 0.84, recall of 0.79, F1-score of 0.81, and ROC AUC of 0.93. The strength of the research is that it combines technical model evaluation with organizational factors such as data governance and interpretability. A limitation is that the case is based on one enterprise fraud platform and therefore may not represent all banking organizations. The article demonstrates that successful fraud detection requires not only accurate models but also data quality, interpretability, governance, and operational support.

\subsection{Systematic Review of Machine-Learning-Based Financial Fraud Detection}
Ali et al.~\cite{ali2022} provide a systematic literature review of machine-learning-based financial fraud detection. The review covers fraud types, machine learning algorithms, datasets, and evaluation metrics. Using the Kitchenham systematic review approach, the authors analyse 93 selected articles. SVM and ANN are identified among frequently used machine-learning approaches, while credit-card fraud is identified as a major fraud category. Important research issues include class imbalance, dataset limitations, evaluation problems, and the need for improved fraud detection methodologies. The review is useful because it provides a systematic classification of existing research and identifies recurring methodological problems. It shapes the current research by demonstrating that algorithm selection should be considered together with datasets and evaluation metrics.

\subsection{Review of Machine Learning Approaches for Credit Card Fraud}
Bin Sulaiman et al.~\cite{binsulaiman2022} review machine learning methods for credit-card fraud detection. The scope includes commonly used machine learning algorithms and the problem of maintaining data confidentiality. The review discusses Artificial Neural Networks, Random Forest, Support Vector Machines, and other approaches, and considers federated learning as a possible way to combine fraud detection with privacy preservation. Major research directions include privacy-preserving and hybrid approaches, while real-time fraud detection remains methodologically difficult. The main strength is the connection between fraud detection and data privacy. A limitation is the specific focus on credit-card fraud. The review contributes to the current research by demonstrating that future banking fraud systems must consider both predictive performance and protection of sensitive financial information.

\subsection{Machine Learning and Deep Learning for Fraud Detection}
Gandhar et al.~\cite{gandhar2024} review machine learning and deep learning techniques for fraud detection. The review organizes approaches into supervised, unsupervised, and reinforcement-learning categories. It examines recent developments in each category and discusses preprocessing and class-balancing approaches such as SMOTE. The authors also consider deep learning for identifying complex fraud patterns. Evolving fraud behaviour, class imbalance, and the need for adaptive models are identified as important research challenges. A strength is the broad coverage of conventional machine learning and deep learning. A limitation is that a broad fraud-detection review may include application domains beyond banking. The article supports the conceptual classification used in this research: supervised classification, unsupervised anomaly detection, and more advanced learning approaches.

\subsection{Systematic Review of AI-Enhanced Credit Card Fraud Detection}
Hafez et al.~\cite{hafez2025} systematically review artificial intelligence approaches to credit-card fraud detection. The scope includes machine learning, deep learning, and meta-heuristic optimization. The review examines more than 30 scholarly articles published between 2019 and 2024 and compares methodologies, datasets, preprocessing strategies, AI techniques, and performance metrics. The authors identify continuing limitations in existing models and emphasize the need for models that can adapt to new fraudulent behaviours. The strength of the review is its recent coverage and structured comparison. Its limitation is the focus on credit-card fraud rather than the full range of banking fraud. It is useful for understanding the evolution from traditional ML to deep learning and optimization-based approaches.

\subsection{Graph Neural Networks for Financial Fraud Detection}
Cheng et al.~\cite{cheng2025} review the application of Graph Neural Networks to financial fraud detection. The review focuses on transaction relationships and the ability of graph models to represent complex financial networks. More than 100 studies are examined and GNN approaches are organized according to applications, deployment environments, and design considerations. Relational modelling is identified as a major direction, while real-world deployment, scalability, and integration into financial systems remain research gaps. The strength of the review is its specific focus on GNNs and relational financial data. Its limitation is that graph methods are more complex to implement than conventional tabular classification. The study expands the understanding of big-data fraud detection by showing that relationships among customers, accounts, devices, merchants, and transactions can be modelled in addition to individual transaction attributes.

\subsection{Literature Review of Machine Learning Techniques for Financial Fraud}
Hernandez Aros et al.~\cite{hernandezaros2024} review financial fraud detection using machine learning techniques. The authors apply PRISMA and Kitchenham methods and analyse 104 articles published between 2012 and 2023. The review examines authors, countries, journals, datasets, fraud types, machine-learning models, and evaluation metrics, and uses bibliometric analysis to identify research patterns. The review finds extensive use of real datasets, with Random Forest among frequently used supervised approaches and autoencoders among unsupervised approaches. It also highlights challenges related to data quality, fraud evolution, dataset availability, and model complexity. Its strength is the large number of reviewed studies and the combination of systematic and bibliometric analysis. A limitation is that the review covers financial fraud broadly rather than only banking transactions.

\subsection{Systematic Review of Fraud Detection in the Banking Sector}
Yanto et al.~\cite{yanto2024} conduct a systematic literature review focused specifically on fraud detection in the banking sector. The review considers Naive Bayes, Logistic Regression, XGBoost, Autoencoder, KNN, LightGBM, Random Forest, SVM, Bidirectional LSTM, and GAN approaches. Data balancing and preprocessing are identified as important factors, while hybrid models are increasingly used. Real-time data and adaptive detection are identified as important future directions. The main strength is its direct focus on banking fraud. A limitation is its dependence on the studies included in the selected open-access database search. The review is particularly useful because it connects algorithm selection directly with banking fraud detection.

\subsection{Big Data Applications in the Banking Sector}
Nobanee et al.~\cite{nobanee2021} review research on big-data applications in banking using bibliometric analysis. The study examines 60 papers published between 2012 and 2020 and identifies themes including investment, profit, competition, credit-risk analysis, banking crime, fintech, and broader use of big data in financial services. The research identifies relatively limited literature on some banking big-data applications and discusses future directions. A strength is the banking-specific big-data perspective. A limitation is that the study is not specifically focused on machine-learning fraud detection and its literature search covers an older period. It is nevertheless important because the proposed research depends on understanding how big-data technologies support banking analytics.

\subsection{Analysis of Machine Learning Algorithms for Online Fraud Detection}
Minastireanu and Mesnita~\cite{minastireanu2019} review research on machine learning algorithms for online fraud detection using a systematic quantitative literature review methodology. The authors construct a hierarchical typology of machine-learning algorithms and examine them using criteria including accuracy, coverage, and cost. The review demonstrates that different algorithms have different advantages and disadvantages and that algorithm selection should depend on the specific requirements of the fraud-detection problem. Its strength is the explicit comparison criteria. Its major limitation is its publication date, but it provides a useful historical foundation for understanding the evolution of algorithm selection in fraud detection.

\subsection{Explainable Artificial Intelligence for Financial Fraud Detection}
Zhou et al.~\cite{zhou2023} propose a user-centered explainable artificial intelligence approach for financial fraud detection. The study is particularly relevant to banking fraud detection because it considers not only the predictive capability of artificial intelligence models but also the need to make fraud-detection decisions understandable to users. The work highlights the importance of explainability and user trust in financial fraud-detection settings. For the current research, this perspective extends the evaluation problem beyond predictive accuracy and toward the practical interpretation of model decisions.

\section{Literature Synthesis and Research Gaps}\label{sec:synth}
The reviewed studies demonstrate several common themes. First, machine learning has become a central method for financial fraud detection. Technical studies repeatedly evaluate supervised classification algorithms including Random Forest, Decision Trees, Gradient Boosting, XGBoost, CatBoost, neural networks, and related models~\cite{mytnyk2023,alsuwailem2023,theodorakopoulos2025,preciado2025}. Review studies similarly identify supervised learning as an important research category~\cite{ali2022,gandhar2024,hernandezaros2024,yanto2024}.

Second, fraud detection is strongly affected by class imbalance. Fraudulent transactions are normally much less frequent than legitimate transactions. Chang et al.~\cite{chang2024} explicitly demonstrate the trade-off between under-sampling and SMOTE, while review studies repeatedly identify imbalance as a major methodological problem~\cite{ali2022,gandhar2024,hafez2025}.

Third, ensemble learning is an important trend. Mytnyk et al.~\cite{mytnyk2023} investigate stacked generalization, while Theodorakopoulos et al.~\cite{theodorakopoulos2025} report strong performance from XGBoost, Random Forest, and CatBoost within a distributed environment. These studies indicate that combining models can be useful, although the resulting systems may require more computational resources and more complex maintenance.

Fourth, the literature increasingly connects machine learning with big-data infrastructure. Theodorakopoulos et al.~\cite{theodorakopoulos2025} demonstrate the use of PySpark for distributed processing, while Byrapu Reddy et al.~\cite{byrapureddy2024} emphasize big-data analytics for large transaction environments. Nobanee et al.~\cite{nobanee2021} provide a broader banking-specific view of how big-data technologies have developed across financial applications.

Fifth, anomaly detection remains important. Bakumenko and Elragal~\cite{bakumenko2022} show the usefulness of machine learning for identifying anomalous financial observations, while review studies identify unsupervised learning and autoencoder-based approaches as important directions~\cite{gandhar2024,hernandezaros2024,yanto2024}. This is particularly important when labelled examples of new fraud types are scarce.

Finally, interpretability and operational factors are becoming increasingly important. Kamal~\cite{kamal2023} demonstrates the relevance of data quality, interpretability, management support, and compliance readiness, while Zhou et al.~\cite{zhou2023} emphasize a user-centered explainable-AI perspective. Recent review literature also identifies graph neural networks, federated learning, hybrid models, and meta-heuristic optimization as emerging approaches~\cite{binsulaiman2022,hafez2025,cheng2025}.

\subsection{Real-Time Detection}
Many studies evaluate machine learning models using historical datasets. However, banking institutions require decisions to be made within a very short time after a transaction is initiated. Future research should therefore investigate real-time streaming architectures in which transaction data can be processed continuously.

\subsection{Class Imbalance}
Class imbalance remains one of the most consistent problems across the reviewed literature. Techniques such as SMOTE, under-sampling, oversampling, and hybrid methods can improve detection, but they can also affect precision and recall. More systematic comparisons of imbalance-handling strategies are required.

\subsection{Concept Drift}
Fraud behaviour changes over time. A model trained on historical fraudulent behaviour may therefore become less effective when new fraud patterns emerge. Continuous learning, adaptive modelling, and model updating are important areas for future investigation.

\subsection{Scalable Big-Data Processing}
Large banks process extremely large transaction streams. Distributed frameworks such as PySpark provide a way to scale model training and inference, but the relationship between model complexity, processing latency, and infrastructure cost requires additional empirical research.

\subsection{Explainability}
High-performing machine-learning models may be difficult for investigators and compliance teams to interpret. Future systems should therefore consider explainability alongside accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, AUC, and latency. The user-centered perspective discussed by Zhou et al.~\cite{zhou2023} is relevant to this requirement.

\subsection{Data Quality and Privacy}
The reviewed literature demonstrates that model performance depends strongly on data quality. At the same time, financial transaction data are highly sensitive. Future research should investigate approaches that improve data quality while preserving privacy and regulatory compliance, including privacy-preserving learning approaches discussed in the review literature~\cite{binsulaiman2022}.

\section{Discussion and Implications for the Proposed Research}\label{sec:disc}
The literature indicates that no single machine learning algorithm is universally appropriate for financial fraud detection. Different models provide different advantages depending on dataset characteristics, fraud type, class imbalance, computational resources, and operational requirements. Traditional supervised algorithms remain important because they can provide strong performance on structured transaction data. Random Forest and gradient-boosting approaches appear repeatedly across the literature. At the same time, deep learning and graph-based approaches are receiving increasing attention because they can model more complex patterns~\cite{gandhar2024,cheng2025}.

Model accuracy alone is not sufficient for a banking fraud-detection system. False positives can increase investigation workload, while false negatives can result in financial losses. Consequently, evaluation should consider precision, recall, F1-score, AUC, specificity, calibration, latency, and computational requirements. The technical literature illustrates this need through the different metrics used by Chang et al.~\cite{chang2024}, Theodorakopoulos et al.~\cite{theodorakopoulos2025}, and Kamal~\cite{kamal2023}.

Big-data technologies provide an important complementary component. The ability to ingest, store, transform, and analyse large transaction streams can make advanced machine-learning models practical in financial environments. The literature therefore supports an integrated research direction in which machine-learning algorithms are combined with big-data processing rather than treating algorithm selection and data infrastructure as separate problems.

For the proposed research, the literature suggests a framework that compares several supervised algorithms, incorporates anomaly-detection methods where appropriate, and evaluates the models using multiple performance metrics. The framework should also consider the infrastructure required to process large transaction volumes efficiently. Data balancing, concept drift, explainability, privacy, and real-time processing should be treated as design considerations rather than as secondary issues.

% ===== METHODS =====
\section{Methods and Materials}\label{sec:methods}
This section describes how the proposed study will be conducted. It translates the research gaps identified in the literature synthesis (class imbalance, concept drift, scalable processing, and explainability) into a reproducible experimental protocol. The section is organised into three parts: the research design (Section~\ref{sec:design}), the data sources and experimental environment (Section~\ref{sec:data}), and the evaluation metrics and success criteria (Section~\ref{sec:metrics}).

\subsection{Research Design}\label{sec:design}
\subsubsection{Overall Research Strategy}
The study adopts a quantitative, experimental, and comparative research design supported by a simulation component. Several machine learning (ML) algorithms and data-balancing strategies are treated as independent variables, while detection quality, calibration, and processing efficiency are treated as dependent variables. The datasets, data splits, random seeds, and hardware are held constant so that observed differences can be attributed to the manipulated factors. The simulation component consists of a synthetic mobile-money dataset that substitutes for confidential bank records and allows the pipeline to be tested at big-data scale.

This design is appropriate for three reasons. First, the research problem is inherently measurable: the effectiveness of a fraud detector is expressed through numerical indicators such as recall, precision, and latency, which calls for a quantitative rather than a qualitative or survey-based approach. Survey-based and mixed designs, such as the one used by Kamal~\cite{kamal2023}, are valuable for studying organisational readiness, but they do not answer the question of which algorithm detects fraud best. Second, the literature consistently shows that no single algorithm is universally superior and that performance depends on data structure and class distribution~\cite{alsuwailem2023,yanto2024,minastireanu2019}; a controlled comparison is therefore needed rather than the evaluation of one model. Third, real banking transactions are highly sensitive and rarely released to researchers~\cite{binsulaiman2022,hernandezaros2024}, which makes public benchmark data and simulated data the only feasible sources for an academic study.

The study is guided by four research questions. RQ1 asks which supervised algorithms achieve the best detection performance on highly imbalanced transaction data. RQ2 asks how imbalance-handling strategies affect the trade-off between precision and recall. RQ3 asks how training time and inference latency scale when the best pipeline is executed in a distributed big-data framework. RQ4 asks whether the decisions of the best model can be explained at the level of individual transactions.

\subsubsection{Methodology}
The workflow follows the Cross-Industry Standard Process for Data Mining (CRISP-DM), adapted to fraud detection in six stages: (i) data acquisition, (ii) preprocessing and feature engineering, (iii) class-imbalance handling, (iv) model training and hyperparameter tuning, (v) evaluation and statistical comparison, and (vi) explainability and scalability analysis. CRISP-DM is selected because it is iterative, technology-neutral, and widely used for applied data-mining projects, which allows the preprocessing and modelling stages to be revisited when evaluation reveals problems.

\subsubsection{Candidate Models}
The model set reflects the algorithms that appear most frequently in the reviewed literature~\cite{theodorakopoulos2025,ali2022,hernandezaros2024,yanto2024}. Logistic Regression (LR) serves as an interpretable baseline. Random Forest (RF) performed strongly on banking transfers~\cite{preciado2025} and financial crime data~\cite{alsuwailem2023}. XGBoost and CatBoost were the best-performing models in the distributed study of Theodorakopoulos et al.~\cite{theodorakopoulos2025}. A Multilayer Perceptron (MLP) represents the neural network family used by Xiong et al.~\cite{xiong2022}. A stacking ensemble, in which a logistic meta-learner combines RF, XGBoost, and CatBoost, follows the stacked generalisation of Mytnyk et al.~\cite{mytnyk2023}. Isolation Forest serves as an unsupervised anomaly detector, motivated by the scarcity of labelled fraud examples discussed by Bakumenko and Elragal~\cite{bakumenko2022}. Graph neural networks~\cite{cheng2025} are excluded from the current scope because the selected datasets do not provide a rich relational structure between customers, devices, and merchants; they are identified as a direction for future work.

\subsubsection{Class-Imbalance Handling}
Three strategies are compared, building on Chang et al.~\cite{chang2024}: (a) no resampling with cost-sensitive class weights, (b) random under-sampling (RUS) of legitimate transactions, and (c) the Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique (SMOTE). Resampling is applied only to the training partition inside each cross-validation fold, never to the validation or test data, to prevent information leakage and optimistic estimates. Combining five supervised models, three balancing strategies, and two datasets yields 30 supervised configurations; with Isolation Forest on both datasets, 32 configurations are evaluated.

\subsubsection{Validation Protocol}
Because fraud patterns change over time, each dataset is split chronologically into training (60\%), validation (20\%), and test (20\%) partitions, rather than randomly. This mirrors deployment, where a model trained on past transactions must classify future ones, and it exposes the effect of concept drift. Hyperparameters are tuned on the training partition by randomised search (50 iterations) with stratified five-fold cross-validation, using the area under the precision-recall curve as the objective. The decision threshold is then selected on the validation partition, and a threshold sensitivity analysis is performed, as in~\cite{kamal2023}. The test partition is used only once, for the final evaluation. Every configuration is repeated with five random seeds, and results are reported as mean $\pm$ standard deviation.

\subsubsection{Explainability and Scalability Analysis}
To address RQ4, SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) are computed for the best model, producing global feature importance and local explanations for a sample of flagged transactions. This responds to the user-centred perspective on explainable fraud detection proposed by Zhou et al.~\cite{zhou2023}. To address RQ3, the best pipeline is re-implemented in Apache Spark using PySpark, following Theodorakopoulos et al.~\cite{theodorakopoulos2025}. It is executed on 10\%, 25\%, 50\%, and 100\% of the large dataset with one, two, and four executors, while training time, throughput, and latency are recorded.

\subsection{Data Sources}\label{sec:data}
\subsubsection{Datasets}
Two open datasets are used (Table~\ref{tab:datasets}). The European credit card dataset contains real transactions made by European cardholders over two days in September 2013. For confidentiality, 28 of its features were transformed by principal component analysis (V1--V28), and only Time and Amount remain in original form. It is the most widely used benchmark in the reviewed literature~\cite{chang2024,theodorakopoulos2025}, which allows the results to be compared with published work.

The PaySim dataset is a synthetic dataset generated by an agent-based simulator calibrated on one month of real logs from a mobile-money service. It contains over six million transactions with interpretable attributes (transaction type, amount, and account balances before and after each transaction). It is used because its size is sufficient to test big-data processing, and its features are interpretable enough to evaluate explanations.

Proprietary bank data are not used because of confidentiality and regulatory restrictions~\cite{binsulaiman2022}, and self-collection of labelled fraud data is not feasible for the research team. Both datasets are publicly available on Kaggle and are downloaded once and stored with their checksums to guarantee reproducibility.

\begin{table*}[!t]
\centering
