\end{table*}

\subsubsection{Data Preparation}
For the credit card dataset, duplicate records are removed, Amount is log-transformed and robust-scaled, and Time is converted into an hour-of-day feature. For PaySim, the transaction type is one-hot encoded and the hour of day is derived as step mod 24. Two balance-error features are engineered to capture inconsistencies typical of fraudulent transfers:
\begin{align}
e_{\mathrm{orig}} &= b^{\mathrm{new}}_{\mathrm{orig}} + a - b^{\mathrm{old}}_{\mathrm{orig}}, \label{eq:eorig}\\
e_{\mathrm{dest}} &= b^{\mathrm{old}}_{\mathrm{dest}} + a - b^{\mathrm{new}}_{\mathrm{dest}}, \label{eq:edest}
\end{align}
where $a$ is the transaction amount and $b$ denotes account balances. Raw account identifiers are dropped and replaced with transaction-count features computed on the training partition only. The isFlaggedFraud field is removed because it is the output of an existing rule-based system and would leak label information. All scalers, encoders, and resamplers are fitted within a single imbalanced-learn pipeline on training data only.

\subsubsection{Software and Hardware}
All tools are open-source (Table~\ref{tab:software}). Models are implemented in Python using scikit-learn, imbalanced-learn, XGBoost, and CatBoost. Experiments on the smaller dataset run on the team's personal laptops, while PaySim and the scalability experiments run on Google Colab and on Spark in local and multi-executor mode. Code is version-controlled on GitHub, and the paper is written in Overleaf.

