\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}\label{sec:metrics}
\subsubsection{Confusion-Matrix Metrics}
Fraud is treated as the positive class. From the numbers of true positives ($TP$), false positives ($FP$), true negatives ($TN$), and false negatives ($FN$), the following measures are computed:
\begin{align}
\mathrm{Accuracy} &= \frac{TP+TN}{TP+TN+FP+FN}, \label{eq:acc}\\
\mathrm{Precision} &= \frac{TP}{TP+FP},\quad \mathrm{Recall} = \frac{TP}{TP+FN}, \label{eq:pr}\\
\mathrm{Specificity} &= \frac{TN}{TN+FP}, \label{eq:spec}\\
F_\beta &= (1+\beta^2)\frac{\mathrm{Precision}\cdot\mathrm{Recall}}{\beta^2\,\mathrm{Precision}+\mathrm{Recall}}, \label{eq:fbeta}\\
\mathrm{MCC} &= \frac{TP\cdot TN - FP\cdot FN}{\sqrt{(TP+FP)(TP+FN)(TN+FP)(TN+FN)}}. \label{eq:mcc}
\end{align}
The F1 score ($\beta=1$) is reported together with F2, which gives recall twice the weight of precision because a missed fraud is usually more costly than a false alert. Accuracy is reported only for comparison with earlier studies: on the credit card dataset, a trivial classifier that labels every transaction as legitimate already reaches 99.83\% accuracy. The Matthews correlation coefficient (MCC) is used as a balanced single-number summary because it uses all four cells of the confusion matrix.

\subsubsection{Threshold-Independent Metrics}
The area under the ROC curve, $\text{ROC-AUC}=\int_0^1 \mathrm{TPR}\,d(\mathrm{FPR})$, is reported because it is used by most technical studies~\cite{mytnyk2023,kamal2023}. However, on highly imbalanced data the precision-recall curve is more informative than the ROC curve. The primary metric of the study is therefore the area under the precision-recall curve, estimated by average precision:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{AP}=\sum_n (R_n-R_{n-1})P_n, \label{eq:ap}
\end{equation}
where $P_n$ and $R_n$ are the precision and recall at the $n$-th threshold.

\subsubsection{Calibration}
Because banks rank alerts by predicted risk, probability quality is measured by the Brier score, also used in~\cite{theodorakopoulos2025}:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{BS}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}(p_i-y_i)^2, \label{eq:brier}
\end{equation}
where $p_i$ is the predicted fraud probability and $y_i\in\{0,1\}$ is the true label.

\subsubsection{Operational and Cost Metrics}
The alert rate $(TP+FP)/N$ estimates the investigation workload highlighted by Kamal~\cite{kamal2023}. A cost measure combines missed fraud and review effort:
\begin{equation}
C=\sum_{i\in FN} a_i + c_{\mathrm{rev}}(TP+FP), \label{eq:cost}
\end{equation}
where $a_i$ is the amount of a missed fraudulent transaction and $c_{\mathrm{rev}}$ is a fixed cost of reviewing one alert. Efficiency is measured by training time, throughput (transactions per second), and median and 95th-percentile inference latency per transaction. For the Spark experiments, speed-up and parallel efficiency are computed as
\begin{equation}
S(n)=\frac{T_1}{T_n},\qquad E(n)=\frac{S(n)}{n}, \label{eq:speedup}
\end{equation}
where $T_n$ is the training time with $n$ executors.

\subsubsection{Statistical Comparison and Explainability}
Differences between models are tested with the Friedman test followed by the Nemenyi post-hoc test, and the two best models are compared on the test set with McNemar's test, at a significance level of $\alpha=0.05$. Explanations are assessed by checking whether the top SHAP features agree with domain knowledge (for example, balance errors in PaySim) and by measuring the stability of feature rankings across seeds with Spearman's rank correlation.

\subsubsection{Success Criteria}
The study is considered successful if the criteria in Table~\ref{tab:success} are met. The thresholds are derived from values reported in the reviewed literature~\cite{mytnyk2023,chang2024,kamal2023}. Together, these metrics evaluate not only whether the models detect fraud accurately, but also whether they are calibrated, efficient, scalable, and interpretable, which the literature identifies as necessary for practical deployment in the banking sector.

\begin{table}[!t]
\centering
\caption{Success criteria.}\label{tab:success}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{2.6cm}p{4.8cm}@{}}
\toprule
Indicator & Target \\
\midrule
PR-AUC (AP) & $\ge$ 10\% relative gain over the LR baseline \\
ROC-AUC & $\ge$ 0.95 \\
Precision / Recall & both $\ge$ 0.80 at the selected threshold \\
