% ===== RESULTS =====
\section{Results}\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Status of the Results and Link to the Research Questions}
The experimental pipeline described in Section~\ref{sec:methods} has not been executed yet, so this section follows the \emph{expected results} option of the assignment. The tables and figures show the structure the final results will have. Every value that is not a documented property of a public dataset is written in italics and marked as expected, or left as TBD, and every expectation is tied to the reviewed literature. None of the italic values is an experimental measurement. Where a dataset property is taken from its public documentation, the text says so.

The section follows the four research questions of Section~\ref{sec:methods}. RQ1 asks which supervised algorithm detects fraud best on highly imbalanced transaction data, RQ2 asks how the imbalance-handling strategies change the balance between precision and recall, RQ3 asks how training time and inference latency scale in a distributed framework, and RQ4 asks whether the decisions of the best model can be explained for individual transactions. The data actually used are described first, followed by one subsection for each question, the additional and error analyses, and the reproducibility details. Interpretation of the results and comparison with other studies are left to the Discussion.

\subsection{Data Used and Deviations from the Plan}\label{sec:res_data}
Table~\ref{tab:data} describes the final datasets. The European credit-card dataset contains 284,807 transactions, of which 492 (0.172\%) are fraudulent, collected over two days in September 2013. Removing exact duplicates, as planned, is expected to leave about 283,726 records with 473 frauds, which means that about 1,081 rows would be removed; the script will confirm these counts. PaySim contains 6,362,620 transactions, of which 8,213 (0.129\%) are fraudulent, spread over 743 hourly steps or roughly 30 simulated days. No PaySim rows are removed, but the \texttt{isFlaggedFraud} column is dropped because it is the output of an existing rule-based system and would leak label information, and raw account identifiers are replaced by transaction-count features computed on the training partition only. The chronological 60/20/20 split gives the partition sizes in Table~\ref{tab:data}. How many frauds fall into each partition depends on when the frauds occur and is therefore TBD.

Before modelling, the script will also check the cleaned data for missing values, constant columns and the class balance of each partition, and these checks will be reported next to Table~\ref{tab:data}. The documentation of the credit-card dataset lists no missing values, which we expect to confirm, and PaySim is generated by a simulator and is expected to be complete as well. Because the split is chronological, the fraud rate of the three partitions can differ from the overall rate, and a partition with very few frauds will be flagged in the text instead of being silently used. Only after these checks will the scalers, encoders and resamplers be fitted on the training partition, as specified in Section~\ref{sec:methods}.

