We report three deviations or clarifications with respect to Assignment~2. The first is that the experiments are not yet run, so expected values replace measured ones. The second concerns the Friedman test, which Section~\ref{sec:methods} defines over datasets. Two datasets are too few blocks for a meaningful test, so we will use dataset--seed pairs as blocks (ten per balancing strategy). Seeds on the same data are not independent, so the resulting $p$-values will be treated as indicative and backed by bootstrap intervals. The third is that the credit-card data cover only 48 hours. The chronological test partition will therefore contain few frauds, probably in the tens, which makes its estimates unstable. For this reason PaySim is the main evidence for RQ1--RQ3, and credit-card results will always be reported with bootstrap 95\% confidence intervals.

\subsection{RQ1: Detection Performance of the Algorithms}\label{sec:res_rq1}
Table~\ref{tab:main} is the main results table. All seven models are evaluated on the same chronological test partition of each dataset, using the balancing strategy and decision threshold chosen on the validation partition. The final cells will contain the mean and standard deviation over five seeds. Because nothing has been measured yet, the table shows expected ranges for the five main metrics and TBD for F2, the Brier score and the 95th-percentile latency. The wide ranges for some models are intentional: they reflect how little the literature tells us about, for example, Logistic Regression on PaySim. Accuracy, specificity and F1 are computed by the same script and will be given in a supplementary table; we omit them here for space and because accuracy alone is uninformative at this class imbalance.

