The expectation that Random Forest, XGBoost and CatBoost will beat the Logistic Regression baseline comes from earlier work reporting strong results for these models on transaction data~\cite{theodorakopoulos2025,preciado2025,kamal2023}. We do not state an expected order among the three, since the ranking of algorithms has been shown to change with the structure of the data~\cite{alsuwailem2023}. The stacking ensemble is expected to match its best base learner or exceed it slightly~\cite{mytnyk2023}, although we do not assume a gain. Isolation Forest uses no labels and is therefore expected to find many frauds at low precision. On the credit-card dataset, the joint precision and recall target of 0.80 is expected to be hard to reach on a chronological test split with few frauds. On PaySim, the balance-error features defined in Eqs.~\eqref{eq:eorig} and~\eqref{eq:edest} are expected to make the target attainable. Figure~\ref{fig:pr} shows the layout of the precision--recall figure that will accompany Table~\ref{tab:main}.

Table~\ref{tab:main} lists one row per model, using the best of the three balancing strategies for that model on the validation partition. The full grid of 32 configurations (five supervised models with three strategies on two datasets, plus Isolation Forest on both datasets) will be given in a supplementary table, so that the choice of strategy behind each row can be checked. Each configuration is trained once per seed on the training partition, the threshold is fixed on the validation partition, and the test partition is scored a single time. In this way the test data play no part in tuning, and every model in the table is evaluated on exactly the same transactions.

Each Spark configuration will be run three times, and the median training time will be used to compute $S(n)$ and $E(n)$, so that a single slow run caused by the shared Colab environment does not dominate a result. Inference latency will be measured on a fixed sample of test transactions after a warm-up run, and throughput will be computed from the total scoring time for the full test partition. The comparison between the single-machine scikit-learn pipeline and the Spark pipeline will be reported only if both give the same predictions up to numerical tolerance; otherwise the difference will be described.

