\subsection{Uncertainty and Statistical Testing}\label{sec:res_stat}
Variability will be reported as the standard deviation over the five seeds. In addition, the best model on each dataset will receive a stratified bootstrap 95\% confidence interval (1,000 resamples of the test partition) for PR-AUC, precision and recall; on the credit-card test partition these intervals are expected to be wide because of the small number of frauds. The Friedman test with Nemenyi post-hoc comparison will be applied to PR-AUC over the ten dataset--seed blocks, and McNemar's test will compare the two best models on the test partition at $\alpha=0.05$. We will call one model better than another only when the corresponding test is significant. Where an interval overlaps or $p\ge0.05$, the difference will be reported as not significant. Test statistics, $p$-values and the critical-difference diagram are TBD, and a null result between the two best boosted models is a realistic outcome that we will report as it is.

\subsection{RQ2: Effect of the Imbalance-Handling Strategies}\label{sec:res_rq2}
Table~\ref{tab:balance} compares cost-sensitive class weights, random under-sampling and SMOTE for the best model from RQ1, with resampling applied only to the training folds. Following Chang et al.~\cite{chang2024}, under-sampling is expected to raise recall at the cost of precision, and SMOTE is expected to sit between the two. Class weights are expected to give the best precision at a similar PR-AUC. These are directions, not predictions of size, and a strategy will be called better only if the paired test supports it. The threshold is re-tuned on the validation partition for every strategy, so differences in precision and recall may partly reflect the threshold and not the resampling itself. This is why PR-AUC, which does not depend on a threshold, is reported next to them.

