\centering
\caption{Balancing strategy and feature ablation for the best model on the PaySim test partition. All cells are TBD; the last column gives the \emph{expected} direction relative to class weights (not measured).}\label{tab:balance}
\footnotesize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{2pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccp{2.3cm}@{}}
\toprule
Configuration & PR-AUC & Prec. & Rec. & Expected vs.\ weights \\
\midrule
Class weights & TBD & TBD & TBD & reference \\
Under-sampling (RUS) & TBD & TBD & TBD & Rec.\ $\uparrow$, Prec.\ $\downarrow$ \\
SMOTE & TBD & TBD & TBD & Rec.\ $\uparrow$ (less), Prec.\ $\downarrow$ \\
No balance-error features & TBD & TBD & TBD & all $\downarrow$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{RQ3: Scalability in Apache Spark}\label{sec:res_rq3}
The best pipeline will be re-implemented in PySpark and run on 10\%, 25\%, 50\% and 100\% of PaySim with one, two and four executors. Figure~\ref{fig:speedup} shows the layout of the speed-up plot; only the ideal linear line is drawn because no timings exist yet. We will record training time, throughput in transactions per second, and median and 95th-percentile inference latency, and compute the speed-up $S(n)$ and efficiency $E(n)$ from Eq.~\eqref{eq:speedup}. On the smaller fractions, start-up and shuffle overhead in Spark are expected to dominate, so near-linear speed-up is expected only on the full dataset, and reaching $E(4)\ge0.7$ is uncertain. Whether the 95th-percentile latency stays under 50 ms is TBD and depends on whether latency is measured per transaction or per micro-batch; the final text will state which.

