\subsection{RQ4: Explainability}\label{sec:res_rq4}
SHAP values will be computed for the best model, giving a global feature ranking and local explanations for a sample of flagged transactions. On PaySim the top features are expected to include the two balance-error features, the transaction amount and the transaction type, because the balance-error features were built to expose exactly these inconsistencies. On the credit-card dataset the ranking is expected to be harder to read, since V1--V28 are PCA components without meaningful names. Stability will be measured by Spearman's $\rho$ between feature rankings across seeds, with a target of $\rho\ge0.8$; the value is TBD. Agreement with domain knowledge will be judged for PaySim only, and credit-card explanations will be described at the level of components.

\subsection{Additional Analyses and Error Analysis}\label{sec:res_add}
We plan three additional analyses. The first is the feature ablation in Table~\ref{tab:balance}, which removes the balance-error features to measure how much they contribute. The second is a threshold-sensitivity analysis on the validation partition, plotting precision, recall and F2 against the decision threshold together with the alert rate $(TP+FP)/N$ and the cost $C$ of Eq.~\eqref{eq:cost}, following~\cite{kamal2023}. The third is a per-group analysis of PaySim, reporting recall by transaction type, since fraud in this simulator occurs only in transfer and cash-out transactions.

Figure~\ref{fig:cm} shows the planned confusion-matrix layout for the best model on each dataset. The error analysis will list the false negatives with the largest amounts, because these dominate the cost $C$, and the highest-scoring false positives, and for each case it will state whether the SHAP explanation points to a systematic cause. We expect, without having verified it, that the hardest frauds are those with small amounts and consistent balances, and that recall may drop on the test partition because of drift relative to the training period. Negative outcomes will be reported without exclusion, including any model that fails to reach the target 10\% relative PR-AUC gain over the baseline.

The cost measure $C$ requires a review cost $c_{\mathrm{rev}}$ per alert, which Section~\ref{sec:methods} describes as fixed but does not give a value. We therefore state this as a clarification of the plan: $c_{\mathrm{rev}}$ will be varied over a range of plausible values, and the ranking of the models by $C$ will be reported for each value, since a single arbitrary value could change the ranking. The alert rate will be reported for the selected threshold of each model. Both quantities are TBD.

\begin{figure}[!t]
\centering
