\noindent\textbf{Reproducibility.}
The code will be released at
\url{https://github.com/iqiiiqqi/assignment3_rmt}.
The planned setup is Python 3.11 with scikit-learn, imbalanced-learn,
XGBoost, CatBoost, and SHAP. Exact package versions will be specified
in \texttt{requirements.txt}, and Apache Spark 3.5 will be used for RQ3.
Experiments will run on laptops with an 8-core CPU and 16 GB of RAM
and on Google Colab. Five random seeds, ranging from 42 to 46, will
be fixed in advance, and both datasets will be stored with checksums.
We expect the credit card experiments to take hours rather than days
on a laptop, while the PaySim and Spark experiments may take longer
on Colab. These are estimates, and measured running times will be
reported after the first full run.

% ===== CONCL =====
\section{Conclusion}\label{sec:concl}
This article reviewed 20 academic studies on the application of machine learning algorithms and big-data analysis to financial fraud detection. Ten technical or research articles were analysed in terms of their problems, methodologies, findings, contributions, strengths, limitations, and relevance, and ten review articles were examined in terms of their scope, trends, methodologies, research gaps, and contribution to the field.

The technical literature shows that Random Forest, XGBoost, CatBoost, neural networks, anomaly detection, and ensemble learning can identify fraudulent financial activity effectively. The review literature shows that fraud detection is an evolving field involving supervised learning, unsupervised learning, deep learning, graph neural networks, federated learning, and hybrid approaches. Class imbalance, concept drift, data quality, explainability, privacy, scalability, and real-time processing remain open challenges, which suggests that future systems should combine accurate models with scalable data-processing architectures and operational requirements rather than focus on predictive accuracy alone.

On this basis we designed a controlled comparison of seven models and three balancing strategies on two public datasets, with chronological validation, SHAP explanations, a Spark scalability study and a multi-metric evaluation. The experiments are still to be run. Section~\ref{sec:results} therefore reports the expected outcomes, clearly marked as such, and the planned reproducibility setup. The next step is to execute the pipeline, replace every expected value by a measured one with its standard deviation or confidence interval and statistical test, and interpret the findings in the Discussion.


